Dos-2-Dos V1.3 ENHANCEMENTS

The following enhancements are now included in Dos-2-Dos, but
are not mentioned in the Dos-2-Dos manual, or are changes to the
use of Dos-2-Dos as described in the manual.

    DF0 Support.  Dos-2-Dos now permits you to use DF0
	as the MS-DOS drive.  Now Dos-2-Dos can be
	used on any Amiga, with or without external
	disk drives.  If you use DF0, you must be careful
	to access AmigaDOS files on some other AmigaDOS
	device, such as RAM:.  Dos-2-Dos displays such a
	warning if you select DF0 as your MS-DOS device.

    Atari ST disk formats supported.  Dos-2-Dos now
	reads, writes, and formats 360KB and 720KB
	Atari ST 3.5-inch diskettes.

    Change MS-DOS drive.  Dos-2-Dos now permits you to
	change the MS-DOS drive selection from within
	Dos-2-Dos.  This means that you do not have to
	exit Dos-2-Dos and restart it to select a
	different MS-DOS disk drive.  Use the RESTART
	command to do this.

    RAM disk and hard disk support.  Dos-2-Dos now
	accepts RAM:, DH0:, and DH1: as valid AmigaDOS
	devices, and displays AmigaDOS directories of
	these devices with proper device names.  See
	discussion below of RAM: usage.

    Large file sizes.  Dos-2-Dos now supports file
	sizes and disk capacities up to 100MB.

    Improved 3.5-inch support.  Dos-2-Dos no longer
	requires that you reboot your Amiga after
	using a 3.5-inch drive for MS-DOS files.
	Other problems relating to accessing 3.5-inch
	drives under AmigaDOS V1.2 have been
	eliminated.

    Format problems.  3.5-inch disks formatted by
	Dos-2-Dos can now be read by IBM systems.
	Dos-2-Dos now places disk parameters into the
	boot block, as required by PC-DOS.

    File dates.  Dos-2-Dos now supports MS-DOS file
	creation dates beyond 1999.


RAM DISK USAGE

Dos-2-Dos supports RAM: as an AmigaDOS device.  This can be very useful
if your Amiga has only a single 3.5-inch drive, which you want to use
for MS-DOS files.  But you should remember that the RAM: device driver
must be loaded into memory before Dos-2-Dos (or any other program) can
access the RAM: device.  Usually the RAM: device is loaded from a
Workbench disk in DF0:.  Therefore, you should force the RAM: device
to be loaded from DF0: before you start Dos-2-Dos.  One way to do this is
to request a directory of RAM:.  Type DIR RAM: to force AmigaDOS to
load the RAM: device and get it going.

If you start Dos-2-Dos and designate DF0: as the MS-DOS drive before the 
RAM: device driver is loaded from DF0:, AmigaDOS will complain that it 
can't find the Workbench disk.  By then it is too late to load the RAM:
device, and you will have to exit Dos-2-Dos to load it.

Please note that all this is unnecessary if you have your RAM: device 
already loaded, or available on another AmigaDOS device, or if you are
using DF1: as the MS-DOS drive.


FORMATTING 3.5-INCH DISKETTES

Dos-2-Dos formats 3.5-inch disks to three user-specified formats: IBM 720KB
(used by the IBM Convertible, Toshiba laptop, and Data General One), Atari
ST 720KB and Atari ST 360KB.  The Atari ST formats are unique to Atari.
Dos-2-Dos reads, but does not format, the Transformer 360KB 3.5-inch format.

The new FORMAT commands for 3.5-inch diskettes are:

	FORMAT (with no parameters) - IBM 720KB (80 track, double sided)
	FORMAT /A		    - ATARI ST 720KB (80 track, double sided)
	FORMAT /A/1		    - ATARI ST 360KB (80 track, single sided)

Format commands for 5.25-inch diskettes are as shown in the Dos-2-Dos manual.


ASCII and WORDSTAR FILE CONVERSION

Dos-2-Dos converts WordStar word-wrap line endings to
"hard" line endings, as required by AmigaDOS text files.  These
"hard" line endings  are interpreted by Scribble! and TextCraft to be
end-of-pararaph markers.  You may wish to manually change these "hard"
line endings to the appropriate "soft" line endings required by your
favorite word processor.

When copying from AmigaDOS to MS-DOS, Dos-2-Dos adds a
carriage return in front of every line feed.  No other
conversions are performed.

If you do not specify the -A option, Dos-2-Dos transfers the
exact number of bytes specified by the file length parameter
of the source file directory entry, with no conversions.


DEFAULT DRIVE and CURRENT DIRECTORY

Dos-2-Dos treats your current AmigaDOS drive and directory as
the default for COPY, TYPE, DIR, and DELETE operations.  This
means that you DO NOT need to include the drive or path if
the current AmigaDOS directory is either the source or
destination of the transfer.

Example:   COPY DF2:*.ASM -A

This command transfers all files with a file type of ASM from
the currently selected directory on DF2 (the MS-DOS drive) to
the current AmigaDOS dirctory (which could be on either DF0
or DF1).  Note the -A option which you need for ASCII files.


MISCELLANEOUS NOTES

There is a quirk (read that BUG) in AmigaDOS V1.1 which
causes it to be confused if you change AmigaDOS directories
from inside Dos-2-Dos and then exit.  AmigaDOS remembers the
directory you are now in, but if you inquire, (using the CD
command) it reports the OLD directory you were in when you
started Dos-2-Dos.  This quirk has been corrected in AmigaDOS
V1.2.

